 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/assets/e164233abcdf9062fd856a100fb7b792912fa030746a63f01ca7fd6683bf7f09.png)" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280059 13194139534167 true] [17592186280058 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534167 true] [17592186280058 1041 "field-1" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280058 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280059 13194139534167 true] [17592186280058 1110 #uuid "82a4dafa-74b6-5c92-b328-33a58173a9f6" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280059 1034 "0" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534167 true] [17592186280059 1111 #uuid "491f86fd-1b83-5929-9ea3-d1af2b627487" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280060 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280061 13194139534167 true] [17592186280060 1089 "q-68105" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280060 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534167 true] [17592186280060 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3).$" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280060 1113 #uuid "28924c15-bb63-5eb8-a04e-6f49ec2c95fe" 13194139534167 true] [17592186280060 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+